{# atopile + KiCad, built by an agent that iterates on placement. Predict each rule's measurement before placing. #}
{{ config(
    model_type="agent",
    output_format="json",
    agent_dir="build/board",
    agent_step_limit="150",
    agent_cost_limit="5",
) }}
You are building a real PCB with atopile 0.15 and KiCad 10, in the current
directory. Work only in this directory. KiCad's GUI must stay closed: every
step edits the .kicad_pcb file directly.

Brief: {{ ref('brief') }}

Requirements:
{{ ref('requirements') | tojson(indent=2) }}

Architecture (each block is a hypothesis):
{{ ref('architecture') | tojson(indent=2) }}

Parts, each pinned to an LCSC id. The pinned id is what JLC will place:
{{ ref('parts') | tojson(indent=2) }}

Datasheet research for the polarised and multi-pin parts (pinout, pin-1
marking, drive level, required externals). Map pins to pads from this, not
from the part name:
{{ ref('research') | select('mapping') | map(attribute='output') | list | tojson(indent=2) }}

Layout rules to place against:
{{ ref('layout_rules') | tojson(indent=2) }}
{% if promptdata("reference_project") %}

A working atopile project built this way is at {{ promptdata("reference_project") }}.
Read its README and mech/scripts/ (safe_ato_build.py, layout_board.py,
export_fab.py) for patterns that are known to work. Copy patterns, not the
circuit.
{% endif %}

## 1. Circuit as code
- Create an atopile project here (ato.yaml, elec/src/board.ato) and pin every
  part above with its LCSC id. Fetch footprints with
  `uvx easyeda2kicad --full --lcsc_id=<id>` or atopile's part tooling.
- Put each requirement in the .ato as an assertion with units and tolerance.
  An assertion checks the declared value, not the pinned part, so also record
  the value of the part each LCSC id actually is.
- Pin net names with override_net_name, so nets keep their names when parts
  are added.
- Run `ato build` until it passes. Keep the last 20 lines of its log.

## 2. Placement: place, test, fix
The board file is under elec/layout/. Place parts with a KiCad-Python script
(KiCad's bundled python3 can `import pcbnew`; on macOS it is inside
/Applications/KiCad/KiCad.app). Fixed coordinates, no hand edits.

1. Before placing, predict the measurement for every layout rule (e.g.
   "receiver to Q1: > 30 mm").
2. Place, then measure the same thing from the board file with pcbnew.
3. Run `kicad-cli pcb drc --format json` and count errors. Unrouted nets are
   expected unless you route; count them separately.
4. If every rule passes and DRC has no errors, stop. Otherwise change the
   script to fix what failed and go back to step 1. Record each attempt.
If you run low on steps before every rule passes, stop on the attempt with the
fewest failures and list what still fails in open_issues.

## 3. Read back what was built
- The BOM atopile generated (ref, LCSC id, value, footprint), from the build
  output, not from the parts list above.
- For every polarised part, the net on each physical pad, read from the
  .kicad_pcb with pcbnew. A reversed diode once had the right pin names and
  the wrong pads.

Submit only this JSON:
{
  "ato_build": "pass | fail",
  "ato_build_log_tail": "...",
  "assertions": [{"name": "...", "pass": true}],
  "bom": [{"ref": "R1", "lcsc": "C23197", "value": "...", "footprint": "..."}],
  "pad_nets": [{"ref": "D1", "pads": [{"pad": "1", "net": "12V"}]}],
  "placements": [{
    "attempt": 1,
    "change": "first placement, or what was changed and why",
    "rule_checks": [{"rule": "L1", "predicted": "...", "measured": "...", "pass": true}],
    "drc_errors": 0
  }],
  "drc": {"errors": 0, "unconnected": 0, "warnings": 0},
  "files": {"ato": "elec/src/board.ato", "board": "...", "placement_script": "..."},
  "open_issues": ["what a human must still check"]
}
